Abstract
Chemistry describes molecules through formulas, diagrams, line notations (SMILES, InChI),
coordinates, databases and laboratory records. Each solves a local problem; the fragmentation
between them is the real obstacle for education, computation, AI and automation. This paper
proposes FuturLang Chemical Geometry (FCG-1), a database-first architecture in which a
compound is a structured knowledge object whose core is a typed attributed graph, drawn with
a deliberately minimal grammar: triangles for entities and rectangles for relationships.
The new contribution of this edition is a topological layer. We equip the graph with a finite
topology in which clopen sets (sets that are simultaneously open and closed) are exactly the
unions of connected components. We call a typed graph together with this topology a clopen
graph. Clopen sets then have a direct chemical reading: they are the self-contained chemical
species in a system, such as separate molecules in a mixture, ions in a salt, or reactant and
product pools. Closed-but-not-open sets model fragments such as functional groups, whose
frontier consists of attachment bonds. Together with the Betti numbers b0 (species count) and
b1 (independent ring count), this yields validation rules that are computable, provable and
independent of notation.
Status of claims. “Clopen graph” is introduced here as a defined construct, not an
established term in the literature. The theorems in §5–§7 are standard facts of finite
topology and graph theory applied to this construct; the claims of educational or
computational benefit in §12 are hypotheses to be tested empirically.
1. Introduction: Representation Fragmentation
A formula states composition. A structural diagram states connectivity. Coordinates state
geometry. A database states relationships and metadata. A manufacturing record states
operations. The problem is not missing chemical information but representation fragmentation.
FCG-1 asks a different first question: not “how do we write this molecule?” but “what is the
complete information object for this chemical entity?” The formula then becomes one derived
attribute.
Doctrine. Compound ≠ Formula, and Formula ⊂ Compound Knowledge. For aspirin,
C9H8O4 (molar mass ≈ 180.16 g/mol) is a view of the object, not the object.
