2. The Chemical Knowledge Object
A compound is the tuple C = (I, N, F, S, G, P, R, Q, Π): identity, names, formula/composition,
structure, graph, properties, rules, quality/validation, and provenance. Its mathematical core is
M = (V, E, τ, β, P, R, Π)
where V is the set of entities, E the relationships, τ : V → T assigns entity types (element,
functional group, molecule, reaction, batch…), β : E → B assigns relationship types (single,
double, triple, aromatic, ionic, semantic…), P assigns properties to V ∪ E, R is a set of rules and
constraints, and Π stores provenance (source, method, date, version, instrument, confidence).
