3. The Underlying Graph and Its Invariants
Let G = (V, E) with V the entity set and E the relationship set. The adjacency matrix A has Aij =
1 when vi and vj are connected, or Aij = bij (bond order) in the order-aware form. Two invariants
matter for everything that follows:
b0(G) = number of connected components, b1(G) = |E| − |V| + b0(G)
b0 counts separate species; b1 (the cycle rank, or “circuit rank”) counts independent rings. For
aspirin with explicit hydrogens, |V| = 21 atoms (C9H8O4) and |E| = 21 covalent bonds (13
between heavy atoms, 8 to hydrogen), so b0 = 1 and b1 = 21 − 21 + 1 = 1, which is the single
benzene ring. Neither number depends on how the molecule is drawn or serialized.
4. A Finite Topology on a Graph
To speak of clopen sets we need open sets. Treat the graph as a finite poset of “cells”: each
edge e lies above its endpoints, written v ≤ e. Declare a set U ⊆ V ∪ E to be open when it is
closed upward: if v ∈ U and v ≤ e, then e ∈ U. This is the Alexandrov (finite, T0) topology of the
face poset, the same construction used for finite models of one-dimensional complexes. The
consequences are immediate:
• A set S is closed exactly when its complement is open, that is, when e ∈ S forces both
endpoints of e into S. The closed sets are precisely the subgraphs (edges never dangle).
• The closure of a set adds the endpoints of its edges; the interior keeps only those vertices all
of whose incident edges are also present.
• The frontier (boundary) of a set S is cl(S) − int(S). For a subgraph, it consists of its vertices
