that still have bonds leaving the subgraph.
Definition (Clopen graph). A clopen graph is a typed attributed graph G together with
the face-poset topology T(G) above. A subset K ⊆ V ∪ E is clopen if it is both open and
closed in T(G).
5. The Clopen Characterization Theorem
Theorem 1. A subset K of V ∪ E is clopen in T(G) if and only if K is a union of connected
components of G (each component taken with all its vertices and edges). Consequently, G
has exactly 2b0(G) clopen sets, and G is connected if and only if its only clopen sets are { }
and the whole graph.
Proof sketch. Suppose K is clopen. Closed means every edge in K brings its endpoints along;
open means every vertex in K brings all its incident edges along. Starting from any element of K
and alternating the two rules, one reaches every vertex and edge of its component, so K
contains whole components. Conversely, a union of components satisfies both rules.
Components partition V ∪ E into b0 blocks, and any subset of blocks gives a distinct clopen set,
hence 2b0. For the final statement, G is connected iff b0 = 1 iff there are 2 clopen sets, { } and
G. Q.E.D.
This is the formal core of the proposal: “is this one molecule or several?” becomes a
topological question with a decidable answer. Clopen sets are the parts of a system that
can be separated with nothing cut and nothing left dangling.
