6. Fragments, Functional Groups and the Frontier
Most chemically interesting sets are not clopen, and the failure is informative. Consider a
functional group F inside a molecule, taken as a subgraph together with its internal bonds. F is
closed because its bonds do not dangle. F is generally not open, because some atom of F
carries a bond to the rest of the molecule. Those bonds are the frontier edges: the attachment
points of the group.
Fr(F) = cl(F) − int(F) = { atoms of F with at least one bond leaving F }
