Proposition 2. For a closed set F (a subgraph) of a connected graph G, F is clopen if and
only if F = { } or F = G. Hence every proper, non-empty functional group in a connected
molecule has a non-empty frontier. A group with frontier size 1 behaves as a monovalent
substituent, size 2 as a linking unit, and so on.
This gives a topological definition of valence of a fragment that is independent of drawing style,
and it is directly computable: it is the number of edges in G with exactly one endpoint in F. For a
carboxyl group in aspirin, the frontier is the single bond to the ring carbon.
