8. Clopen Validation of Compound Objects
The earlier edition defined validity as a conjunction of identity, composition, structure, property
and provenance checks. The topological layer sharpens the structural check. A compound object
declared as n species must satisfy
Vstructure(C) ⇔ b0(G) = n ∧ every edge has both endpoints in V ∧ b1(G) = declared ring
count
FuturLang Chemical Geometry · Clopen Graph Topology · FuturPedia Research Page 4
The middle clause is the closedness of the whole edge set. Composition rules stay as before: the
count of vertices with τ(v) = C must equal 9, with τ(v) = H equal 8 and with τ(v) = O equal 4 for
aspirin. The distinction between stored and validated becomes formal: a stored object that fails
the clopen partition test is rejected before any downstream use, for instance before conversion
to SMILES, where a stray disconnected fragment would otherwise appear silently as a
dot-separated component.
9. Isomers: Why Formula Equality Is Not Identity
Two compounds A and B can satisfy Formula(A) = Formula(B) with A ≠ B. Topology makes the
separation concrete: isomers have identical vertex-type counts but non-isomorphic typed
graphs. The invariants (b0, b1, degree sequence, frontier sizes of recognized groups) are cheap
discriminators; full typed-graph isomorphism (or a canonical form) decides the question. Note
that graph isomorphism cannot distinguish stereoisomers on its own, which is why the property
map P must carry stereochemical and coordinate information. In logic: Formula(A) =
Formula(B) does not imply A = B. A database-first system keeps the distinction by making the
graph primary and the formula derived.
10. Higher-Level Entities: Reactions, Batches and Knowledge
Graphs
Triangles are not limited to atoms. A triangle may stand for a functional group, molecule,
reaction, experiment or batch, and the clopen analysis applies at each level of the hierarchy. A
reaction is R = (S, P, C), with reactants, products and conditions; in the incidence graph a
reaction rectangle joins reactant species to product species. Within a single system snapshot,
reactants and products are clopen sets of the atom-level graph before and after the event.
Atom-mapping across the reaction then records which atoms moved between clopen pools, so
bond breaking and forming are exactly the changes to the component structure of the graph.
Scaling upward, a compound node connects through rectangles to reactions, experiments,
instruments, materials and publications, forming a scientific knowledge graph. The principle
“no scientific fact without provenance” attaches Π to every property, separating measured,
calculated, estimated, imported and proposed values.
