12. Education and Computation
Education. The traditional route is Formula → Molecule. FCG-1 proposes Object → Graph →
Properties → Formula. Students build molecules from triangles and rectangles, then discover
that “one molecule vs. a mixture” is a statement about connected components, that rings are
counted by b1, and that a functional group is a piece with attachment points. This links
chemistry to graph theory, topology and database design.
Computation. One semantic object generates many machine representations: formula,
SMILES, InChI, adjacency matrix, graph-network input, fingerprint, embedding, JSON, SQL and
diagrams. Clopen decomposition is a natural batching step: each clopen component can be
processed, embedded or simulated independently, since by Theorem 1 nothing connects it to
the rest.
Laboratory and manufacturing. The same grammar represents containers, instruments,
batches and tests (for example Batch —USES→ Material, Batch —TESTED_BY→ Analysis). A
hard rule applies throughout: representation is not execution. A compound or reaction
object is not an authorization to make or run anything; any automation link remains under
validated procedures, qualified equipment, access control, safety and quality systems, and
human oversight.
