15. Beyond Chemistry: FuturPedia
The grammar does not depend on chemistry. Gene —EXPRESSES→ Protein, Drug —TARGETS→
Protein, Star —ORBITED_BY→ Planet are all typed graphs, and the clopen analysis again
answers “how many independent systems are present?” Within FuturPedia, FCG-1 serves as the
chemical module of a wider scientific knowledge language in which documents (PDF, DOCX,
HTML, dashboards) are generated views of a graph rather than the source of truth, much as
compiled outputs are views of source code.
16. Limitations
(1) The face-poset topology is a combinatorial model: it captures connectivity, not bond
strength, geometry or electron delocalization, which live in the property map P. (2)
Non-covalent association (hydrogen bonds, ionic lattices, coordination) can be modeled as typed
relationships, but then the choice of which relationship types count as “cutting” a clopen
boundary is a modeling decision that must be declared in the rules R. (3) Hypergraph features,
such as multi-center bonds, require extending the incidence graph, which the rectangle already
supports as an n-ary relationship node. (4) “Clopen graph” is a proposed term and construct;
relation to existing work on finite topological spaces, graph homology and chemical graph
theory should be established in formal peer review.
17. Conclusion
FCG-1 treats a compound as a structured knowledge object, drawn with triangles and
rectangles, stored as a typed attributed graph, and now analyzed topologically. The clopen
graph construct turns three informal chemical ideas into theorems and tests: a species is a
clopen set, a fragment is a closed set with a frontier, and a ring system is counted by b1. The
resulting pipeline is
Object → Graph → Topology → Database → Visualization → Computation → Automation
FCG-1 summary. Primary object: Chemical Knowledge Object. Structure: typed
attributed graph with face-poset topology. Grammar: △ = Entity, ▭ = Relationship.
Principle: database object first; visualization is a view of data; one semantic object, many
machine representations; understand relationships before memorizing representations.
